ntended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**So** substitutes for the positive proposition that they will do it on time.","B":"Not expresses a negative hope, opposite to the prompt.","C":"One substitutes for a count noun, not a whole proposition.","D":"Ones substitutes for plural count nouns, not a proposition."}}},{"id":"0c8d7406-f132-5155-a5ee-de7536222ef2","topicSlug":"ellipsis-substitution","level":"C1","prompt":"“I repair the rowing boat every week.” Add that you do too: “____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"So am I"},{"id":"B","text":"So do I"},{"id":"C","text":"So I do"},{"id":"D","text":"Neither do I"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The first clause is **positive present simple**.","explanation":"Positive present-simple agreement uses **so + do + I**.","keywords":["positive-agreement"],"skill":"positive-agreement","contextKey":"ellipsis-substitution-50","fingerprint":"7ad8cad8cf6ff93f23b043e310a0aa1c110cb30da629be218d9c180e8597d3ee","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ellipsis"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The first clause is **positive present simple**.","Positive present-simple agreement uses **so + do + I**.","Choose **So do I**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Am does not match the ordinary lexical present-simple verb.","B":"Positive present-simple agreement uses **so + do + I**.","C":"So I do acknowledges or confirms a claim rather than adding another matching subject in the requested pattern.","D":"Neither adds agreement to a negative statement, but this one is positive."}}},{"id":"05128325-e1d0-54ec-a5d3-0d99f1236de9","topicSlug":"ellipsis-substitution","level":"C1","prompt":"“I do not repair the rowing boat.” Add that you do not either: “____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Neither am I"},{"id":"B","text":"Neither do I"},{"id":"C","text":"So do I"},{"id":"D","text":"Neither I do"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The first clause is **negative** and uses **do**.","explanation":"Negative agreement uses **neither + do + I**.","keywords":["negative-agreement"],"skill":"negative-agreement","contextKey":"ellipsis-substitution-50","fingerprint":"a8bc341a18a747d645c6c47117764e9c6c9e34bb53bc892d622e07e4b0aec58c","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ellipsis"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The first clause is **negative** and uses **do**.","Negative agreement uses **neither + do + I**.","Choose **Neither do I**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Am does not match the do auxiliary of the original statement.","B":"Negative agreement uses **neither + do + I**.","C":"So adds positive agreement, not agreement with this negative clause.","D":"The agreement pattern inverts the auxiliary and subject."}}},{"id":"b9d3f180-04ee-55c6-ade4-bf54112d6d3a","topicSlug":"ellipsis-substitution","level":"C1","prompt":"“She can repair the rowing boat, and we ____ too.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"have"},{"id":"B","text":"can"},{"id":"C","text":"are"},{"id":"D","text":"do"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Keep the modal **can** while leaving out the repeated action.","explanation":"Retain **can** and omit the recoverable action phrase.","keywords":["auxiliary-ellipsis"],"skill":"auxiliary-ellipsis","contextKey":"ellipsis-substitution-50","fingerprint":"05ef138d8726938eda93c784044cec3be29ab83a89edb2f212e37717340b95de","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ellipsis"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Keep the modal **can** while leaving out the repeated action.","Retain **can** and omit the recoverable action phrase.","Choose **can**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Have does not match the modal construction.","B":"Retain **can** and omit the recoverable action phrase.","C":"Are does not match the can construction.","D":"Do would not preserve the stated modal ability meaning here."}}},{"id":"54176300-39ed-5c5d-a233-bb02ab22fd7e","topicSlug":"ellipsis-substitution","level":"C1","prompt":"“Will they repair the rowing boat on time?” Express a positive hope: “I hope ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"not"},{"id":"B","text":"one"},{"id":"C","text":"ones"},{"id":"D","text":"so"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The missing content is a **whole positive statement**, so use so.","explanation":"**So** substitutes for the positive proposition that they will do it on time.","keywords":["proposition-substitution"],"skill":"proposition-substitution","contextKey":"ellipsis-substitution-50","fingerprint":"10aa48638ffc688b34dca8fcce659082e8cd41c136dd75c77c465e29afb7560d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-ellipsis"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The missing content is a **whole positive statement**, so use so.","**So** substitutes for the positive proposition that they will do it on time.","Choose **so**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Not expresses a negative hope, opposite to the prompt.","B":"One substitutes for a count noun, not a whole proposition.","C":"Ones substitutes for plural count nouns, not a proposition.","D":"**So** substitutes for the positive proposition that they will do it on time."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Leave out recoverable words and use substitutes without losing the intended meaning.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Keep the auxiliary, omit repeated content","explanation":"Ellipsis removes words that the listener can recover from context. After a suitable auxiliary, a repeated verb phrase can be omitted. Match the auxiliary to the clause’s tense and structure.","pattern":"She can swim, and I **can too**.","commonMistakes":["I swim and she is too does not match the auxiliary to the lexical verb in the first clause."],"examples":["“I prepare the visitor guide every week.” Add that you do too: “**So do I**.”","“I restore the old portrait every week.” Add that you do too: “**So do I**.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"So and neither for shared experience","explanation":"Use **so + auxiliary + subject** to add agreement to a positive statement. Use **neither / nor + auxiliary + subject** after a negative one. This is an inverted agreement pattern, not ordinary statement order.","pattern":"I work here. **So do I.** / I don’t. **Neither do I.**","commonMistakes":["So I do is an emphatic acknowledgement, not the same pattern as So do I."],"examples":["“I do not prepare the visitor guide.” Add that you do not either: “**Neither do I**.”","“I do not restore the old portrait.” Add that you do not either: “**Neither do I**.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"One and ones replace count nouns","explanation":"One can replace a singular count noun and ones can replace plural count nouns when the noun is understood. They can combine with adjectives or determiners. They do not freely replace uncountable nouns.","pattern":"I prefer the blue **one**. / The old **ones** are cheaper.","commonMistakes":["Some advice → some, not some ones; advice is uncountable in this use."],"examples":["“She can prepare the visitor guide, and we **can** too.”","“She can restore the old portrait, and we **can** too.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Substitute for whole propositions","explanation":"So can stand for an affirmative proposition after verbs such as think, hope and expect. Negative forms vary by verb: I hope not, I don’t think so. Such can point back to a type of thing already mentioned. Keep the reference unambiguous.","pattern":"Will it work? I **hope so**.","commonMistakes":["I don’t hope so is not the ordinary negative response; use I hope not."],"examples":["“Will they prepare the visitor guide on time?” Express a positive hope: “I hope **so**.”","“Will they restore the old portrait on time?” Express a positive hope: “I hope **so**.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["She can swim, and I **can too**.","I work here. **So do I.** / I don’t. **Neither do I.**","I prefer the blue **one**. / The old **ones** are cheaper.","Will it work? I **hope so**."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["clefts-emphasis"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
